@@@ problem 383 ransom-note
title: Ransom Note
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, String, Counting
params: ransomNote, magazine
time: O(m + n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=magazine.length

=== description
Given two strings `ransomNote` and `magazine`, return `true` if `ransomNote` can be built from the letters of `magazine`, where each letter of `magazine` can be used **only once**.

=== constraints
- `1 <= ransomNote.length, magazine.length <= 10^5`
- both strings consist of lowercase English letters

=== hints
- Count the letters available in `magazine` (26 counters are enough).
- Consume them while reading `ransomNote`; if a counter goes negative, it's impossible.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool canConstruct(string ransomNote, string magazine) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::canConstruct);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string mag = g.lower(n, 6);
        std::string note;
        for (char ch : mag) if (g.coin(0.5)) note += ch;
        auto v = std::vector<char>(note.begin(), note.end());
        g.shuffle(v);
        note.assign(v.begin(), v.end());
        if (note.empty() || g.coin(0.4)) note += (char)('a' + g.irange(0, 7));
        return lc::lines(note, mag);
    });
}

=== approach
Count each letter of `magazine` in an array of 26 integers. Then, for each letter of the note, decrement its count; if a count would drop below zero, the magazine doesn't have enough of that letter. Two linear scans, fixed-size memory.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool canConstruct(string ransomNote, string magazine) {
        int count[26] = {0};
        for (char c : magazine) count[c - 'a']++;       // letters available
        for (char c : ransomNote) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;     // used more than available
        }
        return true;
    }
};

=== examples
"a"
"b"
> false

"aa"
"ab"
> false

"aa"
"aab"
> true

=== tests
"abc"
"cba"
> ?

"z"
"z"
> ?

"aab"
"baa"
> ?

"fffbfg"
"effjfggbffjdgbjjhhdegh"
> ?

@@@ problem 205 isomorphic-strings
title: Isomorphic Strings
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, String
params: s, t
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=s.length

=== description
Two strings `s` and `t` are **isomorphic** if the characters of `s` can be replaced to get `t`, such that:

- every occurrence of a character is replaced by the same character, preserving order;
- no two different characters map to the same character (a character may map to itself).

Return `true` if `s` and `t` are isomorphic.

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^4`
- `t.length == s.length`
- `s` and `t` consist of any valid ASCII characters

=== hints
- You need the mapping in both directions to be consistent.
- Alternatively: record the position where each character was last seen, in both strings; those positions must always agree.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool isIsomorphic(string s, string t) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::isIsomorphic);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string alpha = "abcdefghij";
        std::string s = g.str(n, alpha.substr(0, g.irange(1, 10)));
        auto perm = g.perm(26);
        std::string t;
        for (char ch : s) t += (char)('a' + perm[ch - 'a']);
        if (g.coin(0.4) && n > 0) t[g.irange(0, n - 1)] = 'a' + g.irange(0, 25);
        return lc::lines(s, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Characters are isomorphic-compatible exactly when, at every position, `s[i]` and `t[i]` were **last seen at the same index** (or both never seen). Keep `lastS[256]` and `lastT[256]` storing `index + 1` of the previous occurrence. A mismatch means one string repeats a character where the other doesn't, which breaks the bijection. This checks both directions of the mapping at once.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool isIsomorphic(string s, string t) {
        // last position (+1) at which each character appeared; 0 = never
        int lastS[256] = {0}, lastT[256] = {0};
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); ++i) {
            unsigned char a = s[i], b = t[i];
            if (lastS[a] != lastT[b]) return false;   // histories differ
            lastS[a] = lastT[b] = i + 1;
        }
        return true;
    }
};

=== examples
"egg"
"add"
> true
: 'e' maps to 'a' and 'g' maps to 'd'.

"foo"
"bar"
> false
: 'o' would need to map to both 'a' and 'r'.

"paper"
"title"
> true

=== tests
"a"
"a"
> ?

"ab"
"aa"
> ?

"badc"
"baba"
> ?

"13"
"42"
> ?

@@@ problem 290 word-pattern
title: Word Pattern
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, String
params: pattern, s
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..262144 time=n space=n unit=pattern.length

=== description
Given a `pattern` and a string `s` of space-separated words, decide whether `s` **follows** the pattern: there must be a bijection between the letters of `pattern` and the words of `s`.

- Each letter maps to exactly one word, and each word to exactly one letter.
- No two letters map to the same word and no two words to the same letter.

=== constraints
- `1 <= pattern.length <= 300`
- `pattern` contains lowercase English letters
- `1 <= s.length <= 3000`
- `s` contains lowercase letters and single spaces, with no leading or trailing spaces

=== hints
- Split `s` into words first; if the counts differ, the answer is `false`.
- Keep two maps (letter → word and word → letter) and check both on every step.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool wordPattern(string pattern, string s) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::wordPattern);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string pat = g.lower(std::max(1, n), g.irange(1, 5));
        std::vector<std::string> dict = {"dog", "cat", "fish", "bird", "ant", "bee"};
        g.shuffle(dict);
        std::string s;
        for (char ch : pat) s += (s.empty() ? "" : " ") + dict[ch - 'a'];
        if (g.coin(0.3)) pat[g.irange(0, (int)pat.size() - 1)] = 'a' + g.irange(0, 5);
        if (g.coin(0.1)) s += " dog";
        return lc::lines(pat, s);
    });
}

=== approach
Split `s` into words. If the number of words differs from the pattern length, return `false`. Then walk both sequences together with two hash maps, `letter → word` and `word → letter`. At each position, either both are new (record the pair) or both must already point at each other. Checking both maps enforces a true bijection.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool wordPattern(string pattern, string s) {
        vector<string> words;
        istringstream in(s);
        for (string w; in >> w;) words.push_back(w);
        if (words.size() != pattern.size()) return false;

        unordered_map<char, string> toWord;
        unordered_map<string, char> toChar;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < words.size(); ++i) {
            char c = pattern[i];
            auto a = toWord.find(c);
            auto b = toChar.find(words[i]);
            if (a == toWord.end() && b == toChar.end()) {   // new pair
                toWord[c] = words[i];
                toChar[words[i]] = c;
            } else if (a == toWord.end() || b == toChar.end() || a->second != words[i] || b->second != c) {
                return false;                                // one side disagrees
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};

=== examples
"abba"
"dog cat cat dog"
> true

"abba"
"dog cat cat fish"
> false

"aaaa"
"dog cat cat dog"
> false

=== tests
"a"
"dog"
> ?

"ab"
"dog dog"
> ?

"aaa"
"aa aa aa aa"
> ?

"abc"
"b c a"
> ?

@@@ problem 242 valid-anagram
title: Valid Anagram
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, String, Sorting
params: s, t
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=s.length

=== description
Given two strings `s` and `t`, return `true` if `t` is an **anagram** of `s` (the same letters with the same counts, in any order).

Follow-up: what if the input may contain Unicode characters?

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length, t.length <= 5 * 10^4`
- `s` and `t` consist of lowercase English letters

=== hints
- Sorting both strings works in O(n log n).
- Counting letters works in O(n): +1 for `s`, -1 for `t`, all counts must end at zero.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool isAnagram(string s, string t) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::isAnagram);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string s = g.lower(n, g.irange(1, 26));
        std::vector<char> v(s.begin(), s.end());
        g.shuffle(v);
        std::string t(v.begin(), v.end());
        if (g.coin(0.4) && n > 0) t[g.irange(0, n - 1)] = 'a' + g.irange(0, 25);
        if (g.coin(0.1)) t += 'a';
        return lc::lines(s, t);
    });
}

=== approach
If the lengths differ, they can't be anagrams. Otherwise count letters with one array of 26 integers: add 1 for each letter of `s` and subtract 1 for each letter of `t`. They are anagrams iff every counter ends at zero.

For Unicode input, replace the array with a hash map keyed by code point.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool isAnagram(string s, string t) {
        if (s.size() != t.size()) return false;
        int count[26] = {0};
        for (size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
            count[s[i] - 'a']++;      // letter supplied by s
            count[t[i] - 'a']--;      // letter consumed by t
        }
        for (int c : count)
            if (c != 0) return false;
        return true;
    }
};

=== examples
"anagram"
"nagaram"
> true

"rat"
"car"
> false

=== tests
"a"
"a"
> ?

"a"
"ab"
> ?

"aacc"
"ccac"
> ?

"listen"
"silent"
> ?

@@@ problem 49 group-anagrams
title: Group Anagrams
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Hash Table, String, Sorting
params: strs
time: O(n · k log k)
space: O(n · k)
bench: n=64..262144 time=n space=n unit=number_of_strings_(short_strings)

=== description
Given an array of strings `strs`, group the **anagrams** together. You may return the groups in any order, and the strings inside each group in any order (the judge sorts both).

=== constraints
- `1 <= strs.length <= 10^4`
- `0 <= strs[i].length <= 100`
- `strs[i]` consists of lowercase English letters

=== hints
- Two strings are anagrams iff they are equal after sorting their letters.
- Use the sorted string (or a 26-letter count signature) as a hash map key.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string>& strs) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::groupAnagrams).sort_deep();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::vector<std::string> v;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) v.push_back(g.lower(g.irange(0, 5), 4));
        return lc::lines(v);
    });
}

=== approach
Anagrams become identical when their letters are sorted, so the sorted string is a perfect **group key**. Map each key to the list of strings that produce it, then output the lists.

With `n` strings of length at most `k`, sorting keys costs O(k log k) each, O(n · k log k) in total. (A 26-count signature makes it O(n · k).)

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<string>> groupAnagrams(vector<string>& strs) {
        unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;   // sorted letters -> members
        for (auto &s : strs) {
            string key = s;
            sort(key.begin(), key.end());
            groups[key].push_back(s);
        }
        vector<vector<string>> out;
        out.reserve(groups.size());
        for (auto &[key, members] : groups) out.push_back(move(members));
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
["eat","tea","tan","ate","nat","bat"]
> [["ate","eat","tea"],["bat"],["nat","tan"]]
: "bat" has no anagram in the list; the other words form two groups.

[""]
> [[""]]

["a"]
> [["a"]]

=== tests
["",""]
> ?

["abc","bca","cab","xyz","zyx","q"]
> ?

["ab","ba","abc","a","b"]
> ?

@@@ problem 1 two-sum
title: Two Sum
difficulty: Easy
topics: Array, Hash Table
params: nums, target
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n

=== description
Given an array of integers `nums` and an integer `target`, return the **indices** of the two numbers that add up to `target`.

Each input has **exactly one** solution, and you may not use the same element twice. You may return the two indices in any order (the judge sorts them).

Follow-up: can you do better than O(n²) time?

=== constraints
- `2 <= nums.length <= 10^4`
- `-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9`
- `-10^9 <= target <= 10^9`
- exactly one valid answer exists

=== hints
- For each number `x`, you are looking for `target - x`. How can you find it in O(1)?
- Store the numbers you've already seen in a hash map from value to index.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::twoSum).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        n = std::max(n, 2);
        // Multiples of 4 everywhere except one value = 1 (mod 4) and one = 2 (mod 4):
        // only that pair can sum to 3 (mod 4), so the answer is unique.
        std::vector<int> v(n);
        for (auto &x : v) x = 4 * g.irange(-100000000, 100000000);
        int i = g.irange(0, n - 1), j = g.irange(0, n - 2);
        if (j >= i) ++j;
        v[i] += 1;
        v[j] += 2;
        return lc::lines(v, v[i] + v[j]);
    });
}

=== approach
Scan the array once, keeping a **hash map from value to index** of the elements seen so far. For the current element `x` at index `i`, check whether `target - x` is already in the map; if so, the two indices are the answer. Otherwise store `x → i` and continue.

Checking before inserting guarantees we never pair an element with itself. One pass, O(n) time, O(n) memory.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        unordered_map<int, int> seen;              // value -> index
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
            auto it = seen.find(target - nums[i]); // complement seen earlier?
            if (it != seen.end()) return {it->second, i};
            seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return {};                                 // unreachable: answer guaranteed
    }
};

=== examples
[2,7,11,15]
9
> [0,1]
: nums[0] + nums[1] == 9.

[3,2,4]
6
> [1,2]

[3,3]
6
> [0,1]

=== tests
[1,5]
6
> ?

[-3,4,3,90]
0
> ?

[0,4,3,0]
0
> ?

[-1,-2,-3,-4,-5]
-8
> ?

[1000000000,-1000000000,5]
5
> ?

@@@ problem 202 happy-number
title: Happy Number
difficulty: Easy
topics: Hash Table, Math, Two Pointers
params: n
time: O(log n)
space: O(1)
bench: off

=== description
A number is **happy** if repeatedly replacing it by the sum of the squares of its digits eventually reaches `1`. Numbers that never reach `1` get stuck in a cycle.

Return `true` if `n` is a happy number.

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 2^31 - 1`

=== hints
- The sequence either reaches 1 or loops forever. How do you detect a loop?
- Floyd's cycle detection (a slow and a fast pointer) needs no extra memory.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool isHappy(int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::isHappy);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        return lc::lines(n < 10 ? g.irange(1, 100) : g.irange(1, 2147483647));
    });
}

=== approach
The digit-square-sum sequence is deterministic, so it either reaches `1` or enters a cycle. Use **Floyd's tortoise and hare**: `slow` advances one step, `fast` two steps. If there is a cycle they must meet; the number is happy iff they meet at `1` (1 maps to itself).

After the first step every value is at most 810 (for 10 digits of 9), so the number of steps is small: effectively O(log n) for the first step plus a constant.

=== solution
class Solution {
    // Sum of the squares of the decimal digits.
    int next(int x) {
        int s = 0;
        while (x > 0) {
            int d = x % 10;
            s += d * d;
            x /= 10;
        }
        return s;
    }

public:
    bool isHappy(int n) {
        int slow = n, fast = next(n);
        while (fast != 1 && slow != fast) {   // Floyd's cycle detection
            slow = next(slow);
            fast = next(next(fast));
        }
        return fast == 1;
    }
};

=== examples
19
> true
: 1² + 9² = 82, 8² + 2² = 68, 6² + 8² = 100, 1² + 0² + 0² = 1.

2
> false

=== tests
1
> ?

7
> ?

4
> ?

2147483647
> ?

1111111
> ?

@@@ problem 219 contains-duplicate-ii
title: Contains Duplicate II
difficulty: Easy
topics: Array, Hash Table, Sliding Window
params: nums, k
time: O(n)
space: O(min(n, k))
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n

=== description
Given an integer array `nums` and an integer `k`, return `true` if there are two **distinct** indices `i` and `j` with `nums[i] == nums[j]` and `abs(i - j) <= k`.

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 10^5`
- `-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9`
- `0 <= k <= 10^5`

=== hints
- Only the last `k` elements matter when you look at `nums[i]`.
- Keep a hash set containing exactly the previous `k` values (a sliding window).

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::containsNearbyDuplicate);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        if (n <= 40) return lc::lines(g.ints(n, 0, std::max(2, n / 2)), g.irange(0, n));
        // large: all distinct (forces a full scan), window of n/2
        auto v = g.distinct(n, -1000000000, 1000000000);
        return lc::lines(v, n / 2);
    });
}

=== approach
Maintain a **sliding window** hash set holding the values at indices `i-k .. i-1`. For each `nums[i]`: if it is already in the set, we found two equal values at most `k` apart. Otherwise insert it, and once the window grows beyond `k` elements, remove `nums[i-k]`.

The set never holds more than `k` values, so memory is O(min(n, k)).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsNearbyDuplicate(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
        unordered_set<int> window;                 // values of the last k indices
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)nums.size(); ++i) {
            if (window.count(nums[i])) return true;
            window.insert(nums[i]);
            if ((int)window.size() > k) window.erase(nums[i - k]);   // slide
        }
        return false;
    }
};

=== examples
[1,2,3,1]
3
> true

[1,0,1,1]
1
> true

[1,2,3,1,2,3]
2
> false

=== tests
[1]
0
> ?

[1,1]
0
> ?

[99,99]
2
> ?

[1,2,1]
1
> ?

@@@ problem 128 longest-consecutive-sequence
title: Longest Consecutive Sequence
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Hash Table, Union Find
params: nums
time: O(n)
space: O(n)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=n

=== description
Given an unsorted array of integers `nums`, return the length of the longest sequence of **consecutive** integers (like `4, 5, 6, 7`) that can be formed from its elements.

Your algorithm must run in **O(n)** time.

=== constraints
- `0 <= nums.length <= 10^5`
- `-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9`

=== hints
- Put everything in a hash set.
- Only start counting at numbers that begin a run, i.e. `x - 1` is not in the set. Why does that keep the total work linear?

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::longestConsecutive);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.ints(n, -n, n)); });
}

=== approach
Insert all numbers into a hash set. A number `x` **starts** a run exactly when `x - 1` is not in the set. For each such start, count upward `x+1, x+2, ...` while the numbers exist.

Every number is visited by at most one upward count (the one from its run's start), so the total work is O(n) even though there is a nested loop.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> s(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int best = 0;
        for (int x : s) {
            if (s.count(x - 1)) continue;      // not the start of a run
            int len = 1;
            while (s.count(x + len)) ++len;    // walk the run upward
            best = max(best, len);
        }
        return best;
    }
};

=== examples
[100,4,200,1,3,2]
> 4
: The longest run is 1, 2, 3, 4.

[0,3,7,2,5,8,4,6,0,1]
> 9

[1,0,1,2]
> 3

=== tests
[]
> ?

[5]
> ?

[1,1,1]
> ?

[-1,-2,-3,10,11]
> ?

[1000000000,-1000000000]
> ?
